{"subjectId":"c","id":"unions","title":"Unions","group":"Structures & User-Defined Types","order":36,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <code>union</code> is like a struct whose members all share the <strong>same memory</strong>. It is as large as its largest member, and only one member holds a meaningful value at a time.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nunion Value {\n    int i;\n    float f;\n    char bytes[8];\n};\n\nint main(void) {\n    union Value v;\n    printf(\"sizeof = %zu\\n\", sizeof v);      /* the largest member */\n    v.i = 65;\n    printf(\"i = %d\\n\", v.i);\n    v.f = 2.5f;                               /* overwrites the same bytes */\n    printf(\"f = %.1f\\n\", v.f);\n    printf(\"same address: %d\\n\", (void *)&amp;v.i == (void *)&amp;v.f);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>sizeof = 8\ni = 65\nf = 2.5\nsame address: 1</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tag\"></i>Tagged unions (sum types)</h3>\n<p>Pair a union with an <code>enum</code> tag recording which member is active — the C equivalent of a variant or <code>enum</code> in Rust:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\ntypedef enum { SHAPE_CIRCLE, SHAPE_RECT } ShapeKind;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    ShapeKind kind;                 /* the tag */\n    union {                         /* anonymous union (C11) */\n        struct { double r; } circle;\n        struct { double w, h; } rect;\n    };\n} Shape;\n\ndouble area(const Shape *s) {\n    switch (s-&gt;kind) {\n        case SHAPE_CIRCLE: return 3.14159 * s-&gt;circle.r * s-&gt;circle.r;\n        case SHAPE_RECT:   return s-&gt;rect.w * s-&gt;rect.h;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    Shape shapes[] = {\n        {.kind = SHAPE_CIRCLE, .circle = {1.0}},\n        {.kind = SHAPE_RECT, .rect = {3, 4}},\n    };\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2; i++) printf(\"area = %.2f\\n\", area(&amp;shapes[i]));\n    printf(\"sizeof(Shape) = %zu\\n\", sizeof(Shape));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>area = 3.14\narea = 12.00\nsizeof(Shape) = 24</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary\"></i>Inspecting bytes and endianness</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    union { uint32_t word; uint8_t b[4]; } u = {.word = 0x11223344};\n    printf(\"%02X %02X %02X %02X\\n\", u.b[0], u.b[1], u.b[2], u.b[3]);\n    printf(\"%s-endian\\n\", u.b[0] == 0x44 ? \"little\" : \"big\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>44 33 22 11\nlittle-endian</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Reading a union member other than the one last written (<em>type punning</em>) is allowed in C (it reinterprets the bytes; C++ forbids it). <code>memcpy</code> is the portable alternative in both languages.</p></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>struct</th><th>union</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Each member has its own storage</td><td>All members overlap</td></tr><tr><td><code>sizeof</code></td><td>≥ sum of members (plus padding)</td><td>Largest member (plus padding)</td></tr><tr><td>Active members</td><td>All at once</td><td>One at a time</td></tr><tr><td>Use</td><td>Records</td><td>Variants, memory saving, byte views</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between a structure and a union?</summary><div><p>In a struct every member has its own storage, so all can hold values simultaneously and the size is at least the sum of the members. In a union all members start at the same address and share storage, so the size is that of the largest member and writing one member overwrites the others.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How can you check the endianness of a machine?</summary><div><p>Store a multi-byte integer such as <code>1</code> and inspect its first byte through a <code>unsigned char *</code> or a union: if the first byte is <code>1</code> the machine is little-endian (x86, ARM in its usual mode), otherwise big-endian.</p></div></details></section>"}